# Update README.md on Assignment Changes

Whenever you **add, rename, move, finish, or materially change** an assignment folder, PDF, HTML report, screenshot, LinkedIn/GitHub asset, or other important course file in this repo, **update `README.md` in the same task** before committing.

## When this applies

- New `assignment-N/` folder or new Class/Assignment deliverable
- New or renamed PDF / HTML / `rebuild.sh` / screenshots / post drafts
- Status change (e.g. Draft → Completed)
- New related public links (GitHub repos, LinkedIn posts, course URLs)
- New important root or shared assets that students/mentors should find quickly

## What to update in README.md

1. **Assignments table** — one row per assignment with: number, title, status, links to HTML, PDF, and folder.
2. **Important files** — per-assignment tables listing every submission-critical file with a short description and relative markdown links (`./path/to/file`).
3. **Project / course links** — keep profile, study-guide, and course URLs current.
4. **Folder layout** — refresh the tree if top-level structure changes.
5. **Last updated** date at the bottom (use today’s date from user context).

## Conventions

- Prefer **relative links** for in-repo files so GitHub renders them correctly.
- Keep tables scannable; do not dump every temp file — only HTML, PDF, rebuild scripts, screenshots used in submissions, and key drafts (e.g. LinkedIn post text).
- Mark draft PDFs clearly in the Status column and link text (e.g. `PDF (Draft)`).
- Do not remove older assignment rows when adding new ones; append.

## End of task

After editing README, include it in the same git commit as the assignment/file changes (see `git-end-of-task` rule).
